<h2 id="bootstrap-section-introduction" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="046B">80.1</span> Introduction</h2>

<p>
In this chapter we use the material from the preceding sections to give criteria under which a presheaf of sets on the category of schemes is an algebraic space. Some of this material comes from the work of Artin, see <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/ArtinI">ArtinI</a>]</span>, <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/ArtinII">ArtinII</a>]</span>, <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Artin-Theorem-Representability">Artin-Theorem-Representability</a>]</span>, <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Artin-Construction-Techniques">Artin-Construction-Techniques</a>]</span>, <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Artin-Algebraic-Spaces">Artin-Algebraic-Spaces</a>]</span>, <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Artin-Algebraic-Approximation">Artin-Algebraic-Approximation</a>]</span>, <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Artin-Implicit-Function">Artin-Implicit-Function</a>]</span>, and <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/ArtinVersal">ArtinVersal</a>]</span>. However, our method will be to use as much as possible arguments similar to those of the paper by Keel and Mori, see <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/K-M">K-M</a>]</span>. 
</p>
